\section{Goals for Next Week}{
% Goals: what you want to accomplish next week.
\p{My main goal over the next few months is becoming a [TPU](NZZ7) sensei. To this end, I am working on a [blog](PGP6) traversing deep into the weeds of the TPU ecosystem. I am also working through the [[scaling-book]] with solutions [here](KZR4) and notebook [here](TAAK). Working on some [[060Z]] kernels to hopefully be released some time in the next few months.}
\p{\em{A bit of reflection.} I officially think it is time I focus and specialize. Throughout the past few years, I have had far too diverse interests and trying to learn [\em{everything everywhere all at once}](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Everything_Everywhere_All_at_Once) (never actually seen this movie \smile{}). While I have had a ton of fun learning computer architecture ([here](P3GB) and [here](N7GP)), operating systems ([here](0084)), reinforcement learning, etc. it is simply not optimal to spread myself this thin and continues to lead to burnout. I am big on the idea that it is important to conduct some of [Cal Newport's](calnewport) \em{deliberate practice}. Previously, I was trying to concurrently switch between different things each day, but I am now convinced I need to \strong{minimize cognitive overhead} around decision making / maintenance / conducting said practice. From now on, I will pick one thing ([scaling book](scaling-book) at the time of this), do exercises, make anki cards, then switch after I feel VERY comfortable with the material and continue to maintain this understanding through anki.}
\p{To do so, I will now have two deep work blocks each day of roughly two hours. The first will be this deliberate practice. Over time, we will see if this \em{periodization} approach will be more fruitful than trying to concurrently learn too much.}
}
